[agent:result] execute_statement(
[agent:result]     statement: 'str',
[agent:result]     warehouse_id: 'str',
[agent:result]     *,
[agent:result]     byte_limit: 'Optional[int]' = None,
[agent:result]     catalog: 'Optional[str]' = None,
[agent:result]     disposition: 'Optional[Disposition]' = None,
[agent:result]     format: 'Optional[Format]' = None,
[agent:result]     on_wait_timeout: 'Optional[ExecuteStatementRequestOnWaitTimeout]' = None,
[agent:result]     parameters: 'Optional[List[StatementParameterListItem]]' = None,
[agent:result]     query_tags: 'Optional[List[QueryTag]]' = None,
[agent:result]     row_limit: 'Optional[int]' = None,
[agent:result]     schema: 'Optional[str]' = None,
[agent:result]     wait_timeout: 'Optional[str]' = None
[agent:result] ) -> 'StatementResponse' method of databricks.sdk.service.sql.StatementExecutionAPI instance
[agent:result]     Execute a SQL statement and optionally await its results for a specified time.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     **Use case: small result sets with INLINE + JSON_ARRAY**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     For flows that generate small and predictable result sets (<= 25 MiB), `INLINE` responses of
[agent:result]     `JSON_ARRAY` result data are typically the simplest way to execute and fetch result data.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     **Use case: large result sets with EXTERNAL_LINKS**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     Using `EXTERNAL_LINKS` to fetch result data allows you to fetch large result sets efficiently. The
[agent:result]     main differences from using `INLINE` disposition are that the result data is accessed with URLs, and
[agent:result]     that there are 3 supported formats: `JSON_ARRAY`, `ARROW_STREAM` and `CSV` compared to only
[agent:result]     `JSON_ARRAY` with `INLINE`.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     ** URLs**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     External links point to data stored within your workspace's internal storage, in the form of a URL.
[agent:result]     The URLs are valid for only a short period, <= 15 minutes. Alongside each `external_link` is an
[agent:result]     expiration field indicating the time at which the URL is no longer valid. In `EXTERNAL_LINKS` mode,
[agent:result]     chunks can be resolved and fetched multiple times and in parallel.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     ----
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     ### **Warning: Databricks strongly recommends that you protect the URLs that are returned by the
[agent:result]     `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition.**
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     When you use the `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition, a short-lived, URL is generated, which can be used to
[agent:result]     download the results directly from . As a short-lived is embedded in this URL, you should protect the
[agent:result]     URL.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     Because URLs are already generated with embedded temporary s, you must not set an `Authorization`
[agent:result]     header in the download requests.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     The `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition can be disabled upon request by creating a support case.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     See also [Security best practices].
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     ----
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     StatementResponse contains `statement_id` and `status`; other fields might be absent or present
[agent:result]     depending on context. If the SQL warehouse fails to execute the provided statement, a 200 response is
[agent:result]     returned with `status.state` set to `FAILED` (in contrast to a failure when accepting the request,
[agent:result]     which results in a non-200 response). Details of the error can be found at `status.error` in case of
[agent:result]     execution failures.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     [Security best practices]: https://docs.databricks.com/sql/admin/sql-execution-tutorial.html#security-best-practices
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     :param statement: str
[agent:result]       The SQL statement to execute. The statement can optionally be parameterized, see `parameters`. The
[agent:result]       maximum query text size is 16 MiB.
[agent:result]     :param warehouse_id: str
[agent:result]       Warehouse upon which to execute a statement. See also [What are SQL warehouses?]
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       [What are SQL warehouses?]: https://docs.databricks.com/sql/admin/warehouse-type.html
[agent:result]     :param byte_limit: int (optional)
[agent:result]       Applies the given byte limit to the statement's result size. Byte counts are based on internal data
[agent:result]       representations and might not match the final size in the requested `format`. If the result was
[agent:result]       truncated due to the byte limit, then `truncated` in the response is set to `true`. When using
[agent:result]       `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition, a default `byte_limit` of 100 GiB is applied if `byte_limit` is not
[agent:result]       explicitly set.
[agent:result]     :param catalog: str (optional)
[agent:result]       Sets default catalog for statement execution, similar to [`USE CATALOG`] in SQL.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       [`USE CATALOG`]: https://docs.databricks.com/sql/language-manual/sql-ref-syntax-ddl-use-catalog.html
[agent:result]     :param disposition: :class:`Disposition` (optional)
[agent:result]       The fetch disposition provides two modes of fetching results: `INLINE` and `EXTERNAL_LINKS`.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       Statements executed with `INLINE` disposition will return result data inline, in `JSON_ARRAY`
[agent:result]       format, in a series of chunks. If a given statement produces a result set with a size larger than 25
[agent:result]       MiB, that statement execution is aborted, and no result set will be available.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       **NOTE** Byte limits are computed based upon internal representations of the result set data, and
[agent:result]       might not match the sizes visible in JSON responses.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       Statements executed with `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition will return result data as external links:
[agent:result]       URLs that point to cloud storage internal to the workspace. Using `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition
[agent:result]       allows statements to generate arbitrarily sized result sets for fetching up to 100 GiB. The
[agent:result]       resulting links have two important properties:
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       1. They point to resources _external_ to the Databricks compute; therefore any associated
[agent:result]       authentication information (typically a personal access token, OAuth token, or similar) _must be
[agent:result]       removed_ when fetching from these links.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       2. These are URLs with a specific expiration, indicated in the response. The behavior when
[agent:result]       attempting to use an expired link is cloud specific.
[agent:result]     :param format: :class:`Format` (optional)
[agent:result]       Statement execution supports three result formats: `JSON_ARRAY` (default), `ARROW_STREAM`, and
[agent:result]       `CSV`.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       Important: The formats `ARROW_STREAM` and `CSV` are supported only with `EXTERNAL_LINKS`
[agent:result]       disposition. `JSON_ARRAY` is supported in `INLINE` and `EXTERNAL_LINKS` disposition.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       When specifying `format=JSON_ARRAY`, result data will be formatted as an array of arrays of values,
[agent:result]       where each value is either the *string representation* of a value, or `null`. For example, the
[agent:result]       output of `SELECT concat('id-', id) AS strCol, id AS intCol, null AS nullCol FROM range(3)` would
[agent:result]       look like this:
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       ``` [ [ "id-1", "1", null ], [ "id-2", "2", null ], [ "id-3", "3", null ], ] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       When specifying `format=JSON_ARRAY` and `disposition=EXTERNAL_LINKS`, each chunk in the result
[agent:result]       contains compact JSON with no indentation or extra whitespace.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       When specifying `format=ARROW_STREAM` and `disposition=EXTERNAL_LINKS`, each chunk in the result
[agent:result]       will be formatted as Apache Arrow Stream. See the [Apache Arrow streaming format].
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       When specifying `format=CSV` and `disposition=EXTERNAL_LINKS`, each chunk in the result will be a
[agent:result]       CSV according to [RFC 4180] standard. All the columns values will have *string representation*
[agent:result]       similar to the `JSON_ARRAY` format, and `null` values will be encoded as “null”. Only the first
[agent:result]       chunk in the result would contain a header row with column names. For example, the output of `SELECT
[agent:result]       concat('id-', id) AS strCol, id AS intCol, null as nullCol FROM range(3)` would look like this:
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       ``` strCol,intCol,nullCol id-1,1,null id-2,2,null id-3,3,null ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       [Apache Arrow streaming format]: https://arrow.apache.org/docs/format/Columnar.html#ipc-streaming-format
[agent:result]       [RFC 4180]: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4180
[agent:result]     :param on_wait_timeout: :class:`ExecuteStatementRequestOnWaitTimeout` (optional)
[agent:result]       When `wait_timeout > 0s`, the call will block up to the specified time. If the statement execution
[agent:result]       doesn't finish within this time, `on_wait_timeout` determines whether the execution should continue
[agent:result]       or be canceled. When set to `CONTINUE`, the statement execution continues asynchronously and the
[agent:result]       call returns a statement ID which can be used for polling with
[agent:result]       :method:statementexecution/getStatement. When set to `CANCEL`, the statement execution is canceled
[agent:result]       and the call returns with a `CANCELED` state.
[agent:result]     :param parameters: List[:class:`StatementParameterListItem`] (optional)
[agent:result]       A list of parameters to pass into a SQL statement containing parameter markers. A parameter consists
[agent:result]       of a name, a value, and optionally a type. To represent a NULL value, the `value` field may be
[agent:result]       omitted or set to `null` explicitly. If the `type` field is omitted, the value is interpreted as a
[agent:result]       string.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       If the type is given, parameters will be checked for type correctness according to the given type. A
[agent:result]       value is correct if the provided string can be converted to the requested type using the `cast`
[agent:result]       function. The exact semantics are described in the section [`cast` function] of the SQL language
[agent:result]       reference.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       For example, the following statement contains two parameters, `my_name` and `my_date`:
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       ``` SELECT * FROM my_table WHERE name = :my_name AND date = :my_date ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       The parameters can be passed in the request body as follows:
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       ` { ..., "statement": "SELECT * FROM my_table WHERE name = :my_name AND date = :my_date",
[agent:result]       "parameters": [ { "name": "my_name", "value": "the name" }, { "name": "my_date", "value":
[agent:result]       "2020-01-01", "type": "DATE" } ] } `
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       Currently, positional parameters denoted by a `?` marker are not supported by the Databricks SQL
[agent:result]       Statement Execution API.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       Also see the section [Parameter markers] of the SQL language reference.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       [Parameter markers]: https://docs.databricks.com/sql/language-manual/sql-ref-parameter-marker.html
[agent:result]       [`cast` function]: https://docs.databricks.com/sql/language-manual/functions/cast.html
[agent:result]     :param query_tags: List[:class:`QueryTag`] (optional)
[agent:result]       An array of query tags to annotate a SQL statement. A query tag consists of a non-empty key and,
[agent:result]       optionally, a value. To represent a NULL value, either omit the `value` field or manually set it to
[agent:result]       `null` or white space. Refer to the SQL language reference for the format specification of query
[agent:result]       tags. There's no significance to the order of tags. Only one value per key will be recorded. A
[agent:result]       sequence in excess of 20 query tags will be coerced to 20. Example:
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]       { ..., "query_tags": [ { "key": "team", "value": "eng" }, { "key": "some key only tag" } ] }
[agent:result]     :param row_limit: int (optional)
